=======================================================================
MT8295 Audio DSP userspace loader and demonstrator
==================================================

This is a comparatively tiny python script that works to start and
stop the audio DSP on a Mediatek MT8195 Chromebook, and to read and
write the two shared memory blocks (system DRAM and DSP-local SRAM).

Try, e.g.:

# On the development host (requires a Zephyr SDK in $HOME)
# =======================

make
scp img.bin mt8195-load.py $DUT:

# On the ChromeOS Device
# ======================

# Halt the DSP, any running audio will now fail
./mt8195-load.py stop

# View DRAM as it exists from the now-halted firmware
./mt8195-load.py dump dram | hexdump -C | head -24

# Clobber it with zeros and see that it's modified
dd if=/dev/zero bs=1M count=16 of=zeros.bin
./mt8195-load.py load dram zeros.bin
./mt8195-load.py dump dram | hexdump -C

# Load the new code
./mt8195-load.py load sram ./img.bin

# Start it up and look again
./mt8195-load.py start
./mt8195-load.py dump dram | hexdump -C

=======================================================================
SOF/Zephyr shared memory alternate log stream
=============================================

SOF's logger tool is problematic sometimes.  It requires a custom
kernel build.  You have to get the right LDC (dictionary) file matched
to your firmware.  It uses a heavyweight backend that requries kernel
IPC (and thus fails to survive panics and DSP issues).  It can't be
used from source code that isn't already instrumented with a trace
context (i.e. you can't just "stuff a printf in" to check something
unless your driver is already trying to log stuff).

Sometimes you just want a side channel, so I stole the super-minimal
printf implemention I did for Zephyr's EFI loader and wired it to
unused DRAM memory as a header-only implementation for SOF.  Drop the
mtprintf.h header into src/include, and add one line to initialize the
count (DRAM isn't zero-filled by the firmware loader!) at firmware
entry (patch below), then use "mtprintf()" anywhere in the code you
like.

Read the logs from userspace with "mt8195-load.py log".  There's 1M of
buffer before it runs out of space and things blow up.

--- a/src/init/init.c
+++ b/src/init/init.c
@@ -9,6 +9,7 @@
  * initialisation functions.
  */

+#include <mtprintf.h>
 #include <sof/debug/panic.h>
 #include <sof/drivers/interrupt.h>
 #include <sof/init.h>
@@ -232,6 +233,9 @@ static int primary_core_init(int argc, char *argv[], struct sof *sof)
 #ifndef __ZEPHYR__
 int main(int argc, char *argv[])
 {
+       MTPRINTF_LEN = 0;
+       mtprintf("%s() @ %s:%d\n", __func__, __FILE__, __LINE__);
+
        int err = 0;

        trace_point(TRACE_BOOT_START);
